 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "HILBRE ISLAND. Walk out at low tide, seals say hello, the tide says 'you've got two hours'."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 52,
   "text": "SEAL COVE. Very deep, very private, very much not for your socks."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 6,
   "text": "LEASOWE LIGHTHOUSE. Built 1763, the oldest brick lighthouse in Britain. It has seen things, mostly weather."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 3,
   "text": "WIRRAL WAY. Twelve miles of old railway, fifty years of bicycle. North: Liverpool."
  },
  {
   "x": 28,
   "y": 67,
   "text": "SOUTH: Chester. Roman walls, shops on top of shops. Start walking, stop at the Rows."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "THE DEE ESTUARY. Used to be a port. The sea moved out and left the sand behind. Rude."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 45,
   "text": "PARKGATE. Famous for ice cream, marshes and an incredibly hostile swan."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 7,
   "text": "THURSTASTON COMMON. Gorse, heather and a famous sandstone hill that 'looks a bit like a lump'."
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 54,
   "plaque": "wirral_way_emma_hamilton",
   "title": "Emma, Lady Hamilton",
   "text": "Emma Hamilton was born in 1765 at Ness, just along the shore, the daughter of a blacksmith. She became a famous beauty, the muse of the painter George Romney, the wife of a diplomat and, most famously, the love of Admiral Lord Nelson. Not bad for a lass from the Dee marshes."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 12,
   "plaque": "wirral_way_hand_beast",
   "title": "The Hand Beast",
   "text": "In 1838 quarrymen at Storeton, in the middle of the Wirral, found footprints in the red sandstone left by a reptile some 240 million years ago. The prints looked like human hands, so the maker was named Chirotherium, the 'hand beast'. Nobody has found the beast, just its very tidy handprints."
  }
 ],
